<BaseLayout
  title="Magnesium: Glycinate, Sleep & Deficiency — Nawtch"
  description="Magnesium's well-established role correcting deficiency, an honest look at the thinner sleep/glycinate evidence, dosing, and side effects."
  canonicalPath="/supplements/magnesium/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Magnesium</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Well-established for correcting deficiency, with an early and still-thin case for the sleep benefits it's now sold on.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/supplements/">← Back to Supplements</a>
  </header>

  <p class="field-hint" style="margin:-4px 0 14px;">Not medical advice — talk to a doctor before starting if you have kidney disease or take other medications, since magnesium can affect how some drugs are absorbed.</p>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How It Works</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Magnesium is a cofactor in over 300 enzyme systems — it's involved in protein synthesis, muscle and nerve
        function, blood glucose control, and blood pressure regulation. This deficiency-correction role is
        decades-old, well-established science. The newer marketing angle — magnesium glycinate specifically for
        sleep and relaxation — is a much more recent claim, and it's worth treating those two roles as separate
        claims with very different levels of evidence behind them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">The Research</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Deficiency correction:</strong> per the NIH Office of Dietary Supplements, magnesium's roles in
        muscle contraction, nerve transmission, bone structure, and glucose/blood pressure regulation are
        well-established and supported by decades of research. This is the strong, settled part of magnesium's
        evidence base.
        <br /><br />
        <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Magnesium-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the NIH fact sheet →</a>
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Glycinate and sleep — genuinely preliminary:</strong> a 2025 double-blind, placebo-controlled
        trial in adults reporting poor sleep (n=155) tested 250mg of elemental magnesium as magnesium
        bisglycinate nightly for 4 weeks and found a small but statistically significant improvement in insomnia
        severity scores. It's a real, well-designed trial — but it's also short (4 weeks), relies on subjective
        self-reported outcomes, and stands largely alone rather than as part of a large, consistent body of
        research specific to the glycinate form.
        <br /><br />
        Being honest about the evidence gap here: magnesium's deficiency-correction role has decades of
        research behind it; magnesium glycinate's sleep-specific benefit has a much thinner, newer, and smaller
        evidence base. If you're not magnesium-deficient, the sleep effect (if any) is likely to be modest — this
        is a supplement where the marketing has outpaced the research more than most. If your sleep issue is really
        about timing rather than supplementation, our <a href="/calculators/sleep/">Sleep Cycle Calculator</a> can
        help you find a bedtime or wake-up time that lines up with full 90-minute sleep cycles.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Typical Dosing</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Per the NIH Office of Dietary Supplements, the RDA for adults is roughly 400–420mg/day for men and
        310–320mg/day for women. The sleep trial above used 250mg/day of elemental magnesium as bisglycinate,
        taken in the evening — a reasonable reference point if trying that specific form, though it isn't an
        official dosing guideline the way the RDA is.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Who Should Be Cautious</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        The most common side effect is GI upset — diarrhea, nausea, cramping — driven by unabsorbed magnesium
        drawing water into the gut; this is more common with oxide and citrate forms and less common with
        glycinate/chelated forms, per the NIH fact sheet. Magnesium can reduce the absorption of certain
        antibiotics, including tetracyclines (e.g., doxycycline) and quinolones (e.g., ciprofloxacin,
        levofloxacin) — the NIH recommends spacing doses apart if taking both. Anyone with kidney disease should
        talk to a doctor before supplementing, since impaired kidneys have a reduced ability to excrete excess
        magnesium.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Learn More</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Magnesium-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH Office of Dietary Supplements — Magnesium</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
